Therefore, for practical delivery-time prediction, I would choose MODEL B because the lower RMSE means a smaller prediction error, even though its R² is slightly lower.


TASK 5

Compare two regression models for predicting Spotify song popularity using MSE, RMSE, MAE, and R². Then identify which model is likely overfitting.

CODE:

import numpy as np
from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error, mean_absolute_error, r2_score

# Actual popularity values
y_train = np.array([70, 75, 80, 85, 90, 65, 72, 78, 88, 92])
y_test = np.array([68, 74, 82, 86, 91, 66, 71, 79, 87, 89])

# Model A predictions
model_A_train = np.array([71, 74, 79, 84, 89, 66, 73, 77, 87, 91])
model_A_test = np.array([69, 73, 81, 85, 90, 67, 72, 78, 88, 90])

# Model B predictions
model_B_train = np.array([70, 75, 80, 85, 90, 65, 72, 78, 88, 92])
model_B_test = np.array([55, 90, 70, 95, 75, 80, 60, 88, 72, 98])


# Function to calculate all metrics
def evaluate_model(actual, predicted):
    mse = mean_squared_error(actual, predicted)
    rmse = np.sqrt(mse)
    mae = mean_absolute_error(actual, predicted)
    r2 = r2_score(actual, predicted)

    return mse, rmse, mae, r2


# Evaluate Model A on training data
mse_A_train, rmse_A_train, mae_A_train, r2_A_train = evaluate_model(
    y_train, model_A_train
)

# Evaluate Model A on testing data
mse_A_test, rmse_A_test, mae_A_test, r2_A_test = evaluate_model(
    y_test, model_A_test
)


# Evaluate Model B on training data
mse_B_train, rmse_B_train, mae_B_train, r2_B_train = evaluate_model(
    y_train, model_B_train
)

# Evaluate Model B on testing data
mse_B_test, rmse_B_test, mae_B_test, r2_B_test = evaluate_model(
    y_test, model_B_test
)


# Print results
print("MODEL A")
print("Training MSE:", mse_A_train)
print("Training RMSE:", rmse_A_train)
print("Training MAE:", mae_A_train)
print("Training R²:", r2_A_train)

print("\nTesting MSE:", mse_A_test)
print("Testing RMSE:", rmse_A_test)
print("Testing MAE:", mae_A_test)
print("Testing R²:", r2_A_test)


print("\nMODEL B")
print("Training MSE:", mse_B_train)
print("Training RMSE:", rmse_B_train)
print("Training MAE:", mae_B_train)
print("Training R²:", r2_B_train)

print("\nTesting MSE:", mse_B_test)
print("Testing RMSE:", rmse_B_test)
print("Testing MAE:", mae_B_test)
print("Testing R²:", r2_B_test)


ANSWER:

MODEL B is likely overfitting because its training predictions are almost exactly equal to the actual values, giving extremely low training error and a very high training R² score. However, its test predictions have much larger errors and a lower R² score, showing that the model does not generalize well to unseen data.

MODEL A has more consistent performance between its training and testing results, so it is likely to generalize better.


IMPORTANT METRIC INTERPRETATION:

MSE:
Lower is better. It heavily penalizes large errors.

RMSE:
Lower is better. It represents prediction error in the original unit.

MAE:
Lower is better. It represents the average absolute prediction error.

R²:
Higher is generally better. A value closer to 1 means the model explains more of the variation in the target.

OVERFITTING:
Very good training performance + much worse testing performance usually indicates overfitting.
